  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is upwelling?",
      "a": "Upwelling is when cold, deep ocean water rises to the surface, usually because winds push the surface water away from a coast.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is the sea cold off Peru?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does upwelling bring so many fish?",
      "a": "Deep water is full of nutrients from sunken, rotting life. When it rises into sunlight, phytoplankton bloom, feeding a food chain of small animals, fish, birds and whales.",
      "asks": [
        "Why are upwelling zones good for fishing?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is deep water full of nutrients?",
      "a": "Dead plants, animals and droppings sink and are broken down by bacteria in the deep, releasing nutrients. With no sunlight down there, nothing uses them up until upwelling brings them back.",
      "asks": [
        "Where do ocean nutrients come from?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where does upwelling happen?",
      "a": "Mostly along the western coasts of continents, such as Peru and Chile, California, Namibia and northwest Africa, and also around Antarctica and along the equator.",
      "asks": [
        "Which coasts have upwelling?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What stops upwelling?",
      "a": "If the winds weaken, warm surface water stays put and upwelling slows. During El Niño this happens off Peru, and the anchovy catch can collapse.",
      "asks": [
        "How does El Niño affect upwelling?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Coastal upwelling zones cover only about 1 percent of the ocean's surface but supply a large share of the world's wild fish catch.",
    "The Peruvian anchovy fishery is one of the largest single-species fisheries in the world."
